%erg v1
Title: UX audit: erg user-facing messages, commands, and output semantics
Created: 2026-05-05
Author: haduong

--- log ---
2026-05-05T00:00Z haduong created

--- body ---
Systematic review of every user interaction point in the erg CLI.
Findings are grouped by severity. "Design audit, not a licence to kill" —
each item is a specific, scoped improvement, not a rewrite.

---

## A. Escaped scope from PR #78 (angle brackets in per-command usage strings)

PR #78 fixed `printUsage()` in `main.go` but missed the per-command usage
strings printed when a command receives wrong arguments. These still use
`<arg>` angle brackets:

- `close.go`: `"Usage: erg close <id|file> <reason> [dir]"`
- `validate.go`: `"Usage: erg validate <file ...>"`
- `new.go`: `"Usage: erg new <title> [dir]"`
- `log.go`: `"Usage: erg log <id> <line> [dir]"`

Fix: apply the same UPPER_CASE convention. Scope: 4 one-line string changes.

---

## B. Hardcoded `Author: claude` in generated ticket template

`new.go` writes `Author: claude` into every generated ticket file.
`close.go` writes `claude closed —` and `claude note blocker` into log lines.

These are wrong for any non-Claude author. The field should reflect the
actual actor. Options in priority order:
1. `$(git config user.name)` — available via `os/exec`, matches repo identity
2. `$ERG_AUTHOR` env var — opt-in override
3. Fallback: `haduong` (last resort, still wrong for other users)

Scope: `new.go` template, `close.go` two log-line format strings.

---

## C. Inconsistent warning prefix: `WARNING` vs `WARN:`

`check.go` uses two different prefixes in the same file:
- `"WARNING %s: open ticket in closed/ directory"` (folder-closure)
- `"WARN: Go source files found in tickets/tools/go/ ..."` (stray source)

Standardize to `WARN:` throughout (shorter, matches the stray-source message
added in PR #79, and avoids confusion with the `WARNING:` prefix in
`validate.go` and `ready.go`).

Scope: 2 string changes in `check.go`.

---

## D. `ALREADY_CLOSED` uses underscore; all other status verbs use spaces or none

Status output verbs: `CLOSED`, `ARCHIVED`, `SKIPPED`, `LOGGED` — no underscores.
`ALREADY_CLOSED` breaks the pattern. Options:
- `ALREADY CLOSED` (two words, space) — consistent style
- `CLOSED (already)` — explicit qualifier
- Exit 0 silently (the ticket is already closed; no action needed)

Preferred: `CLOSED (already)` — matches `PASS (3 tickets, 2 warnings)` style.

---

## E. `(s)` pluralization is inelegant

Several messages append `(s)` to fake pluralization:
- `"ERG CHECK: PASS (%d tickets%s)"` where `%s` = `", %d warning(s)"`
- `"ERG VALIDATION: PASS (%d file(s))"`

Use proper pluralization: `1 file` vs `2 files`; `1 warning` vs `2 warnings`.
Small helper: `func plural(n int, word string) string` — returns `"1 file"` or
`"N files"`.

Scope: ~5 format strings + 1 helper function.

---

## F. `erg new` prints filename but no status verb

`new` exits 0 and prints just the filename (e.g., `tickets/0071-foo.erg`).
Every other mutating command prints a status verb first:
`CLOSED`, `ARCHIVED`, `LOGGED`, `SKIPPED`.

`new` should print: `CREATED tickets/0071-foo.erg`

Scope: 1 format string change in `new.go`.

---

## G. `SKIPPED %s (blocking %s)` — ambiguous direction

"SKIPPED 0042-foo.erg (blocking 0039-bar)" means "0042 was skipped because
it is still blocking 0039". But "blocking" is ambiguous: does 0042 block 0039,
or does 0039 block 0042?

Clearer: `SKIPPED %s (still blocks %s — archive %s first)` or
`SKIPPED %s (blocked by open ticket %s)`.

Actually the semantic is: the ticket being archived cannot move because
ANOTHER open ticket has a `Blocked-by:` pointing to it. So:
`SKIPPED %s (needed by %s)` — unambiguous.

Scope: 1 string change in `archive.go`.

---

## H. `version` prints `[unknown — no build metadata]` — too technical

When `buildDate` is empty (binary not built with ldflags):
```
  built:   [unknown — no build metadata]
```
"Build metadata" is internal jargon. Clearer: `[not set — rebuild with make build]`
or simply omit the `built:` line when unknown (the `revision:` line from #0070
will provide context).

---

## I. `init` output uses boolean `%t` for flags and "pointer" as noun

```
- AGENTS.md pointer added: true
- .gitignore entry added: false
```
"Pointer" is a programming term; "entry" is correct. And `true`/`false` in CLI
output is unusual — prefer `yes`/`no` or `added`/`skipped`.

Also: `"- manifest: %s\n"` prints just the path — unclear what the user should
do with it. Suggest: `"- manifest written: %s\n"`.

---

## J. `validate.go` uses "preamble" — undefined jargon

Error messages reference "preamble":
```
'Closed:' header found in log section — only allowed in preamble
```
Users don't know what "preamble" means in this context. Use "header section"
(the part before `--- log ---`).

---

## Triage

**P1 — Fix now (escaped scope or actively wrong):**

| Item | Files | Changes |
|------|-------|---------|
| A | close.go, validate.go, new.go, log.go | 4 strings — per-command usage angle brackets |
| F | new.go | 1 string — add `CREATED` verb |
| G | archive.go | 1 string — `SKIPPED X (needed by Y)` |

→ One PR, ~6 string changes, no logic.

**P2 — Needs design decision:**

| Item | Files | Changes |
|------|-------|---------|
| B | new.go, close.go | Author identity: `git config user.name` → `$ERG_AUTHOR` → `$USER` → `"unknown"` |

→ Separate PR; requires `os/exec` call or `os.Getenv`. Decision: prefer `git config user.name` since erg is always used inside a git repo.

**P3 — Polish sweep (one PR):**

| Item | Files | Changes |
|------|-------|---------|
| C | check.go | `WARNING %s:` → `WARN %s:` |
| D | close.go | `ALREADY_CLOSED` → `CLOSED (already)` |
| E | check.go, validate.go | `plural()` helper + 5 format strings |
| H | selfupdate.go | Omit `built:` line when empty (resolved by #0070) |
| I | init.go | `%t` → `yes`/`no`; "pointer" → "entry"; "manifest:" → "manifest written:" |
| J | validate.go | "preamble" → "header section" (2 strings) |

→ All cosmetic; no logic changes except E (one helper function).
